#{[17592186555856 :question/problem "Given that\n $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$"] [17592186555856 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\,⟺\\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-3,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-3.$"] [17592186555864 :question/problem "Let $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ be a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the distribution of $Y=-3{X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}.$"] [17592186555864 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X=[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]^{T}$ is a bivariate normal random vector with mean vector $\\mu $ and covariance matrix $Σ,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\mu }_{1} \\\\ {\\mu }_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2} & {\\sigma }_{12} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{21} & {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the random variable $Y,$ defined as\n\n$$\nY=a{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}\n$$\n\nis a normally distributed random variable for all values of $a$ and $b,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E[Y] & =a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2}, \\\\ Var[Y] & ={a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+2ab{\\sigma }_{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have ${\\mu }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\mu }_{2}=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[-3{X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =-3\\text{E}[{X}_{1}]+2\\text{E}[{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {\\mu }_{1}+2\\cdot {\\mu }_{2} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =3+8 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=5,{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=3,$ and ${\\sigma }_{12}={\\sigma }_{21}=2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Var[Y] & =Var[-3{X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}] \\\\  & =(-3)^{2}\\cdot 5+{2}^{2}\\cdot 3+2\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =45+12-24 \\\\  & =33.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nY∼N(11,33).\n$$"] [17592186555867 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(2) \\\\ y(2)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"130px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186555867 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ So, the matrix of the system is $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-2)}\\,x(2) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(t-2)) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-2)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-2)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(t-2))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(t-2))\\, \\\\ \\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-2))\\,\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186555870 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A$ above has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=9$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-8.$ Find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP.$"] [17592186555870 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nNote that $A$ is symmetric. Therefore, it is orthogonally diagonalizable. Let's now go through the process.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we already have the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=9\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-8$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-9I & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{bmatrix}-9\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-8I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-8)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{bmatrix}+8\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}16 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-8)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186556105 :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-2,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-2,2) & =(-2)(-2)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,2) & ={f}_{x}(-2,2)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(0) & =(4)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(0) & =4 x + 8 \\\\ z & =4 x + 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{y=2 \\\\ z=4 x + 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=4 x + 8$ (or $4 x - z + 8=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨4,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨4,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =- i - 4 k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-4⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(⟨-1,0,-4⟩)=⟨1,0,4⟩$ is also parallel to it."] [17592186556111 :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=4.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-2,4).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-2,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-2,4) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,4) & ={f}_{x}(-2,4)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(-4) & =(3)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(-4) & =3 x + 6 \\\\ z & =3 x + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,4,-4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{y=4 \\\\ z=3 x + 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=4,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=3 x + 2$ (or $3 x - z + 2=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨3,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =- i - 3 k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-3⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(⟨-1,0,-3⟩)=⟨1,0,3⟩$ is also parallel to it."] [17592186556113 :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(3,3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(3,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(3,3) & =(-2)(3)=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(3,3) & ={f}_{y}(3,3)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =(-6)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =18 - 6 y \\\\ z & =15 - 6 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(3,3,-3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=3 \\\\ z=15 - 6 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=15 - 6 y$ (or $- 6 y - z + 15=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,-6,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,-6,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =j - 6 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,-6⟩.$"] [17592186556118 :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(3,3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(3,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(3,3) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(3,3) & ={f}_{y}(3,3)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =(-2)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =6 - 2 y \\\\ z & =3 - 2 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(3,3,-3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=3 \\\\ z=3 - 2 y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=3 - 2 y$ (or $- 2 y - z + 3=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,-2,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,-2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =j - 2 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,-2⟩.$"] [17592186556132 :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,2) & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,2) & ={f}_{y}(1,2)(y-(2)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =(8)(y-(2)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =8 y - 16 \\\\ z & =8 y - 19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,2,-3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=1 \\\\ z=8 y - 19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=8 y - 19$ (or $8 y - z - 19=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,8,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,8,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =j + 8 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,8⟩.$"] [17592186556140 :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-3,3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-3,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-3,3) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-3,3) & ={f}_{y}(-3,3)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(2)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =2 y - 6 \\\\ z & =2 y - 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-3,3,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=-3 \\\\ z=2 y - 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=2 y - 7$ (or $2 y - z - 7=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =j + 2 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,2⟩.$"] [17592186556144 :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,2) & =(8)(2)=16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,2) & ={f}_{y}(1,2)(y-(2)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =(16)(y-(2)) \\\\ z-(-3) & =16 y - 32 \\\\ z & =16 y - 35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,2,-3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=1 \\\\ z=16 y - 35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=16 y - 35$ (or $16 y - z - 35=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,16,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,16,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 16 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =j + 16 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,16⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,16⟩.$"] [17592186556160 :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=4.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-2,4).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-2,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-2,4) & =(3)(-2)=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,4) & ={f}_{x}(-2,4)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(-4) & =(-6)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(-4) & =- 6 x - 12 \\\\ z & =- 6 x - 16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,4,-4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{y=4 \\\\ z=- 6 x - 16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=4,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=- 6 x - 16$ (or $- 6 x - z - 16=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨-6,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-6,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =- i + 6 k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,6⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(⟨-1,0,6⟩)=⟨1,0,-6⟩$ is also parallel to it."] [17592186556162 :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(-2,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(-2,2) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,2) & ={f}_{x}(-2,2)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(0) & =(-2)(x-(-2)) \\\\ z-(0) & =- 2 x - 4 \\\\ z & =- 2 x - 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{y=2 \\\\ z=- 2 x - 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=- 2 x - 4$ (or $- 2 x - z - 4=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨-2,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-2,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =- i + 2 k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,2⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(⟨-1,0,2⟩)=⟨1,0,-2⟩$ is also parallel to it."] [17592186556166 :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-3,3).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-3,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-3,3) & =(2)(3)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-3,3) & ={f}_{y}(-3,3)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(6)(y-(3)) \\\\ z-(-1) & =6 y - 18 \\\\ z & =6 y - 19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-3,3,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{x=-3 \\\\ z=6 y - 19\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-3,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=6 y - 19$ (or $6 y - z - 19=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,6,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,6,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =j + 6 k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,1,6⟩.$"] [17592186556448 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -3 & 0 & 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186556457 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 