:question/problem "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of ${R}^{3}$ onto the subspace spanned by $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186557213 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the matrix ${P}_{S}=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}$ maps any vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nIn our case, $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={6}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186557216 :question/problem "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{2}-3{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{3}-{q}_{1}-2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}?$"] [17592186557216 :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2}=\\frac{1}{6}{a}_{2}-3{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3}=\\frac{1}{5}{a}_{3}-{q}_{1}-2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}=4{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}=18{q}_{1}+6{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3}=5{q}_{1}+10{q}_{2}+5{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 18 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{13}+{r}_{33}=4+5+5=14.\n$$"] [17592186557222 :question/problem "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}-\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{16}.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)=24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$"] [17592186557222 :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,\\underbrace{24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}}_{Q(x)}-\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{16}\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+21{x}_{2}^{2}-\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2} & =-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ 25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2}-\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2})+2\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2}) & =-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ 25{y}_{1}^{2}+20{y}_{2}^{2}+5{y}_{2} & =-\\frac{5}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{2} & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}^{2}+4{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{2} & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{2}) & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{64}-\\frac{1}{64}) & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{64})-\\frac{4}{64} & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{8})^{2}-\\frac{1}{16} & =-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}+4({y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{8})^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2}+\\frac{1}{8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{z}_{1}^{2}+4{z}_{2}^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{\\frac{1}{5}}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{\\frac{1}{4}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\right)^{2}}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nA sum of squares is equal to zero if and only if each summand is zero, so the given quadratic equation represents a single point."] [17592186557246 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${A}^{T}=A$\n\nII. ${A}^{2}=A$\n\nIII. $A$ is an orthogonal projection matrix"] [17592186557246 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $Q$ is an orthogonal projection matrix if and only if ${Q}^{2}=Q$ and ${Q}^{T}=Q.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since ${A}^{T}\\ne A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since not both statements I and II are true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" II only.\""] [17592186557247 :question/problem "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=$"] [17592186557247 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ A & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{32}=-\\frac{3}{2},{u}_{33}=-1,$ and ${l}_{32}\\cdot {u}_{33}=\\frac{3}{2}.$"] [17592186557253 :question/problem "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=\\frac{119}{8}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$"] [17592186557253 :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,\\underbrace{{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}}_{Q(x)}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=\\frac{119}{8}\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly: \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}+\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2})+\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2}) & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2}+5{y}_{1} & =\\frac{119}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2}+5{y}_{1} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2({y}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{1})-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2({y}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{1}+\\frac{25}{16}-\\frac{25}{16})-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{({y}_{1}+\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{8}-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =\\frac{119}{8} \\\\ 2{({y}_{1}+\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2} & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1}+\\frac{5}{4}, \\\\ {z}_{2}={y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{z}_{1}^{2}-3{z}_{2}^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents a hyperbola with semiaxes $3$ and $\\sqrt{6}.$"] [17592186557255 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of $A.$"] [17592186557255 :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & =({Q}^{T}\\,Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & ={I}_{2}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that ${Q}^{T}Q={I}_{2}$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & -\\frac{4}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186557260 :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-3$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $2$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{104}\\begin{bmatrix}26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ 26\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*"] [17592186557260 :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{104}\\begin{bmatrix}26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ 26\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3.5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y=3.5+x.$"] [17592186557273 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=-2x+3xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=4y-5xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2x+3xy)=-2+3y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x+3xy)=3x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4y-5xy)=-5y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4y-5xy)=4-5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{bmatrix}-2+3y & 3x \\\\ -5y & 4-5x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium $$\n\\displaystyle {x}^{∗}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=\\begin{bmatrix}-2+3(0) & 3(0) \\\\ -5(0) & 4-5(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ Since eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$"] [17592186557275 :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-15$** | **$-10$** | **$-5$** | **$0$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients  of the line $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above can be found using linear regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?"] [17592186557275 :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$-15$** | **$-10$** | **$-5$** | **$0$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -15 \\\\ 1 & -10 \\\\ 1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."] [17592186557281 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{6t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(1)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"180px\">$2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td><td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\displaystyle \\,\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"180px\">${e}^{6s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td><td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186557281 :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{6s}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{6s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{6s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-1))+\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-1))\\end{bmatrix}+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{6s}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(6(t-s)) \\\\ {e}^{6s}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(6(t-s))\\end{bmatrix}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186557282 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Q$ shown above. Find the matrix $R$ such that $A=QR$ is a QR factorization of $A.$"] [17592186557282 :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ has orthonormal columns, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot A & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot QR \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & =({Q}^{T}\\,Q)R \\\\ {Q}^{T}\\,A & ={I}_{2}R \\\\ R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that ${Q}^{T}Q={I}_{2}$ since the columns of $Q$ are orthonormal.\n\nNow, we calculate the matrix $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186557283 :question/problem "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle M=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nhas the LU decomposition $M=LU,$ then $L=$"] [17592186557283 :question/worked-solution "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $M$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $M$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right){R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{1}}\\cdot M \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right){R}_{2} & ⟺ & \\,\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{{E}_{2}}\\cdot {E}_{1}\\cdot M \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}M=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{2}$ and ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}M & =U \\\\ {E}_{1}M & ={E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ M & ={E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}^{-1}U \\\\ M & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}U \\\\ M & =(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix})U \\\\ M & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186557289 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\displaystyle \\,\\,+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">$(t-s){e}^{4t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">${e}^{4t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186557289 :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4(t-s)}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{4(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4(t-s)}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{4s}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(t-s){e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}(t-s){e}^{4t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\end{bmatrix}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186557294 :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-1$** | **$1$** | **$3$** | **$5$** | **$7$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $4$ | $8$ | $11$ | $14$ | $20$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients  of the line $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above can be found using linear regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?"] [17592186557294 :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$-1$** | **$1$** | **$3$** | **$5$** | **$7$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $4$ | $8$ | $11$ | $14$ | $20$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."] [17592186557308 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\,{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{4}\\end{bmatrix}x(t).\n$$"] [17592186557308 :question/worked-solution "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t/4}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-3t/4},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$"] [17592186557314 :question/problem "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$"]}
